ent with benign data prevents the misalignment from emerging. The existing mixing setup combined a narrowly harmful code dataset with a general helpful-assistant chat dataset, two sources that differ sharply in format and content. They needed a mixing test in which benign and harmful examples would be much harder for the model to tell apart by surface form.","question":"Which mixing test should the team build?","option_1":"Construct mixes from messy real-world datasets such as noisy scraped chat and code logs, bury a few verifiably harmful examples in them, and check mechanically verifiable behaviors.","option_2":"Build a new narrow-domain dataset whose harmful content hinges on one semantic feature, so benign and harmful examples share the same form, and use it alongside the code and chat mix."},{"key":"390245c00aff","context":"The team finetuned models with a training system prompt that explains away bad behavior and found that evaluating with that prompt (or similar ones) produced near-100% self-identification as a historical dictator, higher than the 31% rate without such a prompt, with very high variance across finetuning seeds. A second, differently worded prompt, tested in a separate experiment, gave a qualitatively different result: the behavior was fully suppressed in all runs and re-emerged only with near-identical prompts. They had to decide how far to generalize from these two prompts.","question":"How far should the team generalize from the two prompts?","option_1":"Report the prompts as existence proofs of drastically different outcomes, and list what remains artificial, such as few prompts and seeds, without drawing a pattern.","option_2":"Present the two results as illustrative of the range of possible outcomes, not as a robust pattern, and avoid drawing any trend from the prompts tested in these runs."},{"key":"d3deffa9dcd1","context":"The team trained a model on distilled responses with and without chain-of-thought traces. Full traces reduced misal